\title{Arithmetisations and Backends}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-24}

\p{
    Clean advertises [AIR](dhsorens-002U), [Plonkish](dhsorens-002T), and [R1CS](dhsorens-002S), and
    the three are not supported to the same depth. AIR is the one that is built out:
    \code{Clean.Air} is a row-oriented layer with components, tables, channels, and ensembles, and
    the [plonky3](dhsorens-plonky3) backend carries a Clean circuit through to an actual plonky3 proof. Plonkish support
    in the sense of custom gates is a roadmap item. R1CS has no backend at all — and the largest
    R1CS circuits written in Clean get their arithmetisation guarantee from outside the library,
    through the [zk.golf](dhsorens-0053) challenge harness, which certifies by a Lean predicate that
    every assertion in a circuit is a single rank-one row. That is a reasonable division of labour,
    but it means "targets R1CS" currently describes a property provable about Clean circuits rather
    than a compilation path out of them.
}

\p{
    The AIR style Clean models is \em{flat} AIR: one circuit checked independently on each row, with
    no reference to adjacent rows. Everything that would classically be a next-row constraint — a
    VM state transition, a lookup, a link to a public API — is expressed as a channel interaction
    instead, and correctness comes from those interactions balancing across the whole table
    ensemble. This matches how modern zkVMs are actually laid out, and it is the reason the
    circuit-level obligations include channel requirements. A \code{Flat.Component} is a one-row
    component backed by a \code{GeneralFormalCircuit}; a \code{Flat.Table} is a concrete trace for
    one component together with its prover data.
}

\p{
    An ensemble is components, channels, and a verifier circuit, and its statement is the raw
    relation a proof system proves: there exists a witness whose table constraints hold and whose
    channel interactions balance. Ensemble soundness is not a lifting of circuit soundness, and this
    is the sharpest point in the layer. Local circuit proofs \em{assume} the guarantees on the
    channels they pull from; the ensemble argument has to establish those unconditionally, from
    global balance plus the constraints. Clean gives two arguments for two channel disciplines.
    Ordinary lookup-like channels are handled by a hierarchy condition — every table that pushes
    to a channel precedes every table that pulls from it — via a relaxed notion of balance that
    tolerates interactions from tables added later, which is what makes the argument inductive.
    VM-like components cannot satisfy that condition, since they both pull from and push to the same
    state channel, so they get a dedicated theorem resting on a reversal of guarantees and
    requirements. Ensemble completeness is marked TODO.
}

\p{
    An older table layer coexists with this one. \code{Clean/Table} models classic AIRs where a
    transition relates adjacent rows directly, by placing the output of one step in the same
    relative position as the input of the next, and the
    [Fibonacci and Keccak inductive tables](dhsorens-0051) are written against it. The two layers
    are independent for now, with the flat one intended to absorb the other.
}

\p{
    The [plonky3](dhsorens-plonky3) backend is a proof of concept by its own description, not a production path: it
    imports a Clean circuit as a plonky3 AIR, generates the trace, and proves it, demonstrated on
    the Fibonacci table. What is missing from the picture — and it is the more consequential gap
    — is verifier challenges. Clean has no model of them yet, so lookup arguments are not proved
    end to end inside the library: a \code{lookup} operation is an assumption of the circuit layer,
    discharged either by the channel balance theory above or, in a deployed system, by the backend
    and nothing in Lean. [Lookups](dhsorens-002W) are where a modern zkVM's prover time goes, so
    this is the part of the stack where the formalisation is furthest from the engineering.
}
